   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the criminal procedure law, in relation to the duties of the district attorney",
    "basePrintNo": "S6406",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6406-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §440.50, CP L",
    "lawSection": "Criminal Procedure Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6406\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the criminal procedure law, in\nrelation to the duties of the district attorney\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\n\nThis bill would require district attorneys to provide the Board of\nParole with a copy of the written notice it provides to a crime\nvictim, or his/her family, regarding the disposition of a criminal\nmatter, and the victim's right to be heard by the Board in connection\nwith the offender's possible release to parole.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 of the bill would amend Criminal Procedure Law § 440.50(1)\nto require that the office of the prosecuting district attorney\nprovide the Board of Parole with a copy of the written notice provided\nto victims of felony offenses under Article 125 of the Penal Law, or\nviolent felony offense under section 70.02 of the Penal Law, or other\noffenses under Article 130.\n\nSection 2 would make this law effective immediately.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe Board of Parole currently only becomes aware of a crime victim's\nimpact statement when the victim, or his/her representative,\nindependently contacts the Department of Corrections and Community\nSupervision and provides such a statement. Unless and until that\ncontact occurs, apart from what may be reflected in the county\npre-sentence investigation report, the Board lacks useful information\nabout the victim, their current whereabouts and what they may or may\nnot know about their statutory right to be heard by the Parole Board.\nThis has resulted in instances where a victim only learns about an\ninmate's possible release after the inmate has appeared before the\nBoard and been granted parole.\n\nThis bill would enable the Board to gain access to information about\nall crime victims so that the Board can readily address matters\nassociated with their possible submission of victim impact statements\nfor its consideration. With this information and the Board's early\ninteraction with victims or their families, the Board would be assured\nthat in instances where an inmate serving a sentence for a homicide,\nsex offense or violent felony offense is being considered for parole,\nthe victim or victim's representative have been contacted and apprised\nof their right to be heard in connection with the inmate's possible\nrelease.\n\nThis bill would also resolve a discrepancy between sections 440.50(1)\nand 440.50(2) to include the notification to a victim of a felony\noffense under Article 130 of the Penal Law. Although violent felony\noffenses pursuant to Section 70.02 of the Penal Law encompass some sex\noffenses, it does not include all felony offenses under Article 130.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis proposal was previously introduced as A.6945 (2015).\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nThere is no fiscal impact to the State. District attorney offices\nwould incur the costs associated with First Class mail.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
